WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.217
Bread for dinner comes from the bakery round the corner, ordered by phone.

2
00:00:07.217 --> 00:00:09.842
It's a lovely bakery with a terrible phone.

3
00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:14.176
Sometimes the call drops halfway, and sometimes nobody picks up at all.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.211
So the cook has a routine.

5
00:00:16.211 --> 00:00:16.837
No bread?

6
00:00:16.837 --> 00:00:18.021
Crackers instead.

7
00:00:18.021 --> 00:00:21.781
Or ring again, after a short pause, then a longer one.

8
00:00:21.781 --> 00:00:24.567
And meanwhile, plates can drop anywhere.

9
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:29.229
Here's the bread course as a flow, a recipe card for values.

10
00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:38.113
The cook sends out yesterday's loaf, then rings the bakery inside withTimeout of 50 milliseconds, which gives up if the call takes longer.

11
00:00:38.113 --> 00:00:41.912
Below her, catch sends out crackers if anything goes wrong.

12
00:00:41.912 --> 00:00:44.487
Today the bakery takes 100 milliseconds.

13
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.876
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

14
00:00:47.876 --> 00:00:55.136
A timeout is a cancellation, a CancellationException: a coroutine's way of saying, stop, you've been called off.

15
00:00:55.136 --> 00:00:57.600
And catch doesn't handle cancellation.

16
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.257
So doesn't the timeout fly straight past?

17
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:03.960
It doesn't.

18
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.978
The diner gets yesterday's loaf, then crackers, and the caller sees nothing.

19
00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:10.827
The catch heard the timeout.

20
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:15.898
So the rule we all learned, catch ignores cancellation, is a little too tidy.

21
00:01:15.898 --> 00:01:18.236
Let's end the bread course five ways.

22
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:26.540
The bakery call throws an IOException, Java's usual exception for a failed call, like a dropped line.

23
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:27.224
Crackers.

24
00:01:27.224 --> 00:01:30.414
The bakery throws a CancellationException.

25
00:01:30.414 --> 00:01:31.554
Crackers again.

26
00:01:31.554 --> 00:01:32.922
The timeout fires.

27
00:01:32.922 --> 00:01:33.605
Crackers.

28
00:01:33.833 --> 00:01:35.605
Now the diner drops a plate.

29
00:01:35.605 --> 00:01:41.804
The catch does nothing, and the caller sees an IllegalStateException: the diner dropped the plate.

30
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:45.639
And last, the caller's own job is cancelled while it waits.

31
00:01:45.639 --> 00:01:50.345
The catch does nothing again, and the caller sees a JobCancellationException.

32
00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:51.720
Here's the picture.

33
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.754
A net is strung under the cook's side of the line.

34
00:01:54.754 --> 00:01:59.367
Everything above catch is called the upstream: the cook and her bakery call.

35
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:04.951
Everything below it is the downstream: the diner, the collector, and whoever called collect.

36
00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:11.172
Anything that falls from above lands in the net, even a cancellation, while the job is still running.

37
00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:14.680
A plate the diner drops falls below the net, to the caller.

38
00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.437
And when the caller calls off the whole dinner, the net steps aside, on purpose.

39
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:23.442
But an exception doesn't carry a label saying who threw it.

40
00:02:23.442 --> 00:02:24.914
So how does catch know?

41
00:02:24.914 --> 00:02:27.089
Let's build it by hand, in Kotlin.

42
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:34.528
First it wraps the downstream emit and remembers anything that comes out: the diner's dropped plate.

43
00:02:34.528 --> 00:02:38.373
Then, when the whole collect fails, it asks two questions.

44
00:02:38.373 --> 00:02:40.030
Did the diner throw this?

45
00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:40.825
Throw it on.

46
00:02:40.825 --> 00:02:43.013
Is it our own job's cancellation?

47
00:02:43.013 --> 00:02:44.206
Throw that on too.

48
00:02:44.206 --> 00:02:46.592
Everything else goes to your action.

49
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:53.063
Remember the inspector from lesson 2, who refuses any emit after the diner has thrown?

50
00:02:53.063 --> 00:02:59.429
That rule is called exception transparency: an exception from downstream belongs to downstream.

51
00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:01.507
So catch must never swallow it.

52
00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:06.060
The net is generous, but it only covers what hangs above it.

53
00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.611
Serving inside collect is below the net.

54
00:03:08.611 --> 00:03:14.669
To protect it, move the serving into onEach, above catch, and call collect with nothing inside.

55
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.971
And a caught error still ends the upstream.

56
00:03:17.971 --> 00:03:23.113
map fails on 2, catch emits minus 1, and the diner gets 1, then minus 1.

57
00:03:23.113 --> 00:03:24.541
The 3 is never made.

58
00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:25.909
So here's the rule.

59
00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:28.433
catch hears only what falls from above it.

60
00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:30.717
Put the work it should cover above it.

61
00:03:31.433 --> 00:03:33.462
The net can send out crackers.

62
00:03:33.462 --> 00:03:36.505
Sometimes you'd rather ring the bakery again.

63
00:03:36.505 --> 00:03:37.384
That's retry.

64
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.537
The grocer's van brings a delivery note: flour, eggs and butter, read line by line.

65
00:03:43.537 --> 00:03:47.874
On the first try, the line drops after eggs, with an IOException.

66
00:03:47.874 --> 00:03:50.876
The pantry reads the note through retry of 3.

67
00:03:50.876 --> 00:03:52.811
What does the pantry receive?

68
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.340
Flour, eggs, flour, eggs, butter.

69
00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.187
Two lines went in twice.

70
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:08.101
A retry collects the upstream again, and a cold flow is a recipe card: collecting it again cooks it again, from the top.

71
00:04:08.101 --> 00:04:13.299
So retry of 3 means up to four collections, and whatever went out goes out again.

72
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.035
The flour doesn't mind.

73
00:04:15.035 --> 00:04:19.508
The pantry book does: it now says we bought twice the flour we did.

74
00:04:19.508 --> 00:04:24.715
So whatever comes after a retry must be idempotent: safe to see an item twice.

75
00:04:24.715 --> 00:04:27.585
Skip any line whose id you've already seen.

76
00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.848
Now the cook's pauses.

77
00:04:29.848 --> 00:04:35.266
Ringing a bakery that won't answer a thousand times a second isn't a routine.

78
00:04:35.266 --> 00:04:36.532
It's a prank call.

79
00:04:36.532 --> 00:04:37.799
So the waits grow.

80
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.284
That growing wait is a backoff.

81
00:04:40.284 --> 00:04:45.074
In code, it's a delay inside retryWhen, the operator under retry.

82
00:04:45.074 --> 00:04:53.401
It gets the cause, and an attempt number that starts at 0, so the waits are 100 milliseconds, then 200, then 400.

83
00:04:53.633 --> 00:05:00.385
Against a bakery that never answers, she rings at 0, 100, 300 and 700 milliseconds.

84
00:05:00.385 --> 00:05:05.103
Then she gives up, and the original IOException comes out.

85
00:05:05.333 --> 00:05:15.754
Reactor's backoff rings at the same times, but wraps the last error in a RetryExhaustedException, and outside tests adds jitter, a small random spread.

86
00:05:15.754 --> 00:05:18.445
In Flow, you write the policy yourself.

87
00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:21.719
Position matters for the egg timer too.

88
00:05:21.719 --> 00:05:24.467
A timeout above the retry limits each try.

89
00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:27.869
Below it, a timeout limits them all, waits included.

90
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.852
So, does a timeout fly past catch?

91
00:05:30.852 --> 00:05:31.051
No.

92
00:05:31.051 --> 00:05:33.568
catch doesn't sort exceptions by type.

93
00:05:33.568 --> 00:05:34.761
It asks who threw.

94
00:05:34.761 --> 00:05:42.909
What falls from the cook's side lands in the net, timeouts included; the diner's plate and the caller's own cancel go past.

95
00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:46.155
And a retry cooks the recipe again, from the top.

96
00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:47.306
Quick check.

97
00:05:47.306 --> 00:05:50.204
catch sits above map, and map throws.

98
00:05:50.204 --> 00:05:51.379
Who handles it?

99
00:05:54.367 --> 00:05:55.391
Not that catch.

100
00:05:55.391 --> 00:06:00.033
map is below the net, so its exception falls past it, to the caller.
